          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The 2-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
            is naturally equipped with the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            with symplectic form its canonical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/volume+form"} volume form
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
          %p
            From the point of view of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanical+system"} quantum mechanical system
            that describes a rigid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rotor"} rotor
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            in 3-dimenensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euclidean+space"} Euclidean space
            (and just the rotation/spin degrees of freedom, not the translational degrees of freedom). Accordingly, the coresponding
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            carries a natural
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angular+momentum"} angular momentum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operators"} quantum operators
            that generate the rotation of the sphere.
          %p
            More abstractly in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coadjoint+orbit"} coadjoint orbit
            of the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+Lie+algebra"} special unitary Lie algebra
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+group"} special unitary group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔰𝔲
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathfrak{su}(2)/SU(2)
            (and hence of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+group"} spin group
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(3)
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "spin+group#ExceptionalIsomorphisms"} exceptional isomorphisms
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            of the 2-sphere is a special case of the general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+method"} orbit method
            for producing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representations"} irreducible representations
            of suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+groups"} Lie groups
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian+action"} Hamiltonian action
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SU
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2)
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+operators"} quantum operators
            acting on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} space of quantum states
            with an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible representation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SU(2) \simeq Spin(3)
          %h2#details Details
          %p
            The following walks through all the ingredients and steps of the geometric quantization of the 2-spere. Several of the ingredients/steps described are not specific to the 2-sphere but apply generally in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+polarization"} Kähler polarization
            quantization, but are spelled out again for completeness. Similarly, much of the discussion is really about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+geometry"} complex geometry
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
          %h3#the_symplectic_geometry The symplectic geometry
          %p
            We introduce first the 2-sphere with its canonical structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K%C3%A4hler+manifold"} Kähler manifold
          %h4#coordinate_charts Coordinate charts
          %p
            For all of the following it is useful to choose an adapted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+charts"} coordinate charts
            on the 2-sphere, namely that induced by regarding it as the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
          #RiemannSphere.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              Choose any point
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo ∈
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{\infty} \in S^2
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 - \{x_\infty\}
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeomorphism"} diffeomorphic
              to the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℝ
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+plane"} complex plane
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{C} \simeq U_{+} \coloneqq (S^2 - \{x_\infty\}) \hookrightarrow S^2
            %p
              is one
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
              on the 2-sphere. Accordingly, removing the antipodal point
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ≔
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x_0 \coloneqq 0 \in \mathbb{C} \simeq U_+
            %p
              yields another
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+chart"} coordinate chart
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ≔
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{C} \simeq U_{-} \coloneqq (S^2 - \{x_0\}) \hookrightarrow S^2
                    \\,.
            %p
              We denote the canonical complex coordinate function on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mo −
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_{+/-}
              by
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi z
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mo −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_{+/-}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mo −
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbb{C} - \{0\})
                    \\simeq
                    S^2 - \{x_0, x_\infty\} \simeq (U_+ - \{x_0\})
                    \\to
                    (U_- - \{x_\infty\})
                    \\simeq
                    (S^2 - \{x_0, x_\infty\})
                    \\simeq
                    (\mathbb{C} - \{0\})
            %p is given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo ↦
                    %msup
                      %mi z
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    z \mapsto z^{-1}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Hence on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2 - \{x_0, x_\infty\}
              the two coordinate functions are complex inverses of each other
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mo +
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mo −
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mn 1
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    z_+ = (z_-)^{-1}
                    \\,.
            %p
              This defines an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
              by two open charts which defines the 2-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
              as a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+function"} holomorphic function
              this moreover gives
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^2
              the structure of a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifold"} complex manifold
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
          %p
            In the following we often just write “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z
            ” for “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_+
            ” or “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_-
            ” when the chart
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_+
            or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mo −
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_-
            is understood.
          %p By the canonical embedding
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S^2 \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}^3
          %p
            into the 3-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            as the unit 2-sphere given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo {
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi x
                      %mn 3
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 3
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
